course: dictionary
lesson: 10321
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Trade has more than one meaning, so context tells you which one the writer means. Ask yourself what is happening in the sentence, then pick the meaning that fits.
explain: «In 1608 the French founded Québec and traded furs with First Nations by canoe across huge distances.» The clues are furs, canoes and huge distances. Here trade means swapping or selling goods between people.
explain: Trade can also be a job. «Her father is a carpenter by trade.» Swap your meaning into the sentence: "a carpenter by job" makes sense, so trade means a job that needs skill with the hands.
explain: Some sentences use trade for buying and selling between whole countries. «Arabic became the shared language of learning and trade.» Check it: "the shared language of buying and selling" fits, so this is the same meaning as the first sentence, but on a bigger scale.
fact: Trade means buying and selling goods between people, shops or countries.
fact: To trade means to swap one thing for another, like trading stickers at break time.
fact: A trade is a job that needs skill with the hands, like a plumber or an electrician.
fact: A trade can also be a single swap or deal.
fact: Beaver fur was so valuable for hats in Europe that the beaver became a symbol of Canada.
exercise: «Ella traded her red marble for two blue ones.» What does traded mean here? | Lost in the rain | Built a wall with stones | Bought a train ticket | *Swapped one thing for another
exercise: «Sam is a plumber by trade.» What does trade mean here? | *A job that needs skill with the hands | A single swap or deal | A dark, quiet room | Buying and selling goods
exercise: «The Silk Road was a famous trade route.» What is a trade route? | A school bus route | A road only for cars | *A path along which goods travel between places | A kind of football match
test: «Sam will trade his bike for a scooter.» What does trade mean here? | Buying a house | *To swap one thing for another | A job that needs skill with the hands | A quiet, empty room
test: «The fair trade shop sells goods that were made by people who are paid a fair price.» What is the best meaning of trade here? | A single swap or deal | A plumber's job | *Buying and selling goods between people | To lose something on purpose
exam: Jayden says trade always means buying and selling, so it can only be a verb. Is he right? | *No, trade can also mean a swap or a job that needs skill with the hands | Yes, and it is always a verb | No, trade is a colour | Yes, trade only ever means buying and selling
